---
title: "The logistic map and period doubling"
description: "The one-line model $x_{n+1} = r x_n (1 - x_n)$, whose orbits settle, oscillate, double their period again and again, and turn chaotic."
date: 2026-08-29
categories: [maps, bifurcations, chaos]
---


@may1976 argued that even the simplest nonlinear models deserve careful study, because their behavior can be astonishingly rich. The standard example is the logistic map
$$
x_{n+1} = f_r(x_n) = r\,x_n\,(1 - x_n), \qquad x_n \in [0, 1], \quad 0 \leq r \leq 4.
$$ {#eq-logistic}

## Fixed points and the first doubling

::: {#prp-fixed}
For $1 < r < 3$, the fixed point $x^* = 1 - 1/r$ of @eq-logistic is stable, and it loses stability at $r = 3$.
:::

::: {.proof}
A fixed point of a map is stable when $|f'(x^*)| < 1$. Here $f_r'(x) = r(1 - 2x)$, so $f_r'(1 - 1/r) = r(2/r - 1) = 2 - r$, and $|2 - r| < 1$ exactly when $1 < r < 3$.
:::

At $r = 3$ the multiplier passes through $-1$. Orbits near $x^*$ then alternate from side to side, and a stable orbit of period 2 appears.

::: {#prp-two-cycle}
The period-2 orbit $\{p, q\}$ of @eq-logistic exists for $r > 3$ and is stable for $3 < r < 1 + \sqrt 6 \approx 3.449$.
:::

::: {.proof}
The points $p, q$ are the roots of $f_r(f_r(x)) = x$ other than the fixed points, namely the roots of $r^2x^2 - r(r+1)x + (r+1) = 0$. These are real and distinct exactly when $r > 3$. They satisfy $p + q = (r+1)/r$ and $pq = (r+1)/r^2$. The multiplier of the cycle is
$$
f_r'(p) f_r'(q) = r^2 (1 - 2p)(1 - 2q) = r^2 \bigl(1 - 2(p + q) + 4pq\bigr) = -r^2 + 2r + 4.
$$
This equals $1$ at $r = 3$ and $-1$ at $r = 1 + \sqrt 6$, and lies strictly between them for $3 < r < 1 + \sqrt 6$.
:::

## Try it: cobweb diagram

A cobweb diagram iterates the map graphically: go up to the curve, across to the diagonal, and repeat. Try $r = 2.8$ (spiralling into the fixed point), $r = 3.2$ (a 2-cycle), $r = 3.5$ (a 4-cycle) and $r = 3.9$ (chaos).

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
viewof r = Inputs.range([2.5, 4], {value: 3.2, step: 0.001, label: "r"})
viewof x0 = Inputs.range([0.01, 0.99], {value: 0.2, step: 0.01, label: "x₀"})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
cobweb = {
  const f = (x) => r * x * (1 - x);
  const pts = [{x: x0, y: 0}];
  let x = x0;
  for (let i = 0; i < 80; i++) {
    const y = f(x);
    pts.push({x, y}, {x: y, y});
    x = y;
  }
  return pts;
}
curve = d3.range(0, 1.0001, 0.005).map((x) => ({x, y: r * x * (1 - x)}))
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
Plot.plot({
  className: "phase-plot",
  width: Math.min(width, 560),
  height: Math.min(width, 560),
  x: {domain: [0, 1], label: "xₙ"},
  y: {domain: [0, 1], label: "xₙ₊₁"},
  marks: [
    Plot.line([{x: 0, y: 0}, {x: 1, y: 1}], {x: "x", y: "y", stroke: "currentColor", strokeOpacity: 0.4}),
    Plot.line(curve, {x: "x", y: "y", stroke: "currentColor", strokeWidth: 2}),
    Plot.line(cobweb, {x: "x", y: "y", stroke: "currentColor", strokeWidth: 0.9, strokeOpacity: 0.75})
  ]
})
```

## The cascade

The 2-cycle itself doubles to a 4-cycle, then an 8-cycle, and so on. The doublings accumulate at $r_\infty \approx 3.5699$, beyond which most parameter values give chaotic orbits (@fig-bifurcation). The chaotic region is broken by periodic windows. The widest begins at $r = 1 + \sqrt 8 \approx 3.828$ with a stable 3-cycle.

::: {#fig-bifurcation}

::: {#d4712b16 .cell renderings='["light","dark"]' execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
r = np.linspace(2.5, 4.0, 2000)
x = np.full_like(r, 0.5)
for _ in range(1000):
    x = r * x * (1 - x)
R, Xs = [], []
for _ in range(250):
    x = r * x * (1 - x)
    R.append(r)
    Xs.append(x)
R, Xs = np.concatenate(R), np.concatenate(Xs)

def draw(fig, ax, c):
    ax.scatter(R, Xs, s=0.05, lw=0, color=c.accent, alpha=0.5, rasterized=True)
    for rv in (3, 1 + np.sqrt(6), 1 + np.sqrt(8)):
        ax.axvline(rv, color=c.muted, lw=0.6, ls=":")
    ax.set(xlim=(2.5, 4), ylim=(0, 1), xlabel=r"$r$", ylabel=r"$x$")

themed(draw, figsize=(8, 4.2))
```

